Loop-gain (stability) analysis "stb" in ngspice
===============================================

The stb analysis gives the loop gain of a feedback loop over frequency,
and from it the phase margin and the gain margin. The loop stays closed:
the operating point is that of the working circuit, and nothing has to
be cut, biased or loaded by hand. The loop is named by a probe, which is
a 0 V voltage source in the loop or a terminal of an instance.

At each frequency the small-signal circuit is solved for a unit voltage
in series with the probe and for a unit current into its node. The two
responses are combined into the return ratio T of the loop after

  M. Tian, V. Visvanathan, J. Hantgan, K. Kundert, "Striving for
  small-signal stability", IEEE Circuits and Devices Magazine,
  vol. 17, no. 1, 2001.

The result holds for a loop that carries signal both ways through the
probe, and it does not depend on the impedances on the two sides.

Example decks are in examples/stb.


1  Syntax
---------

  .stb <probe> dec|oct|lin <points> <fstart> <fstop> [localgnd=<node>] [diag=1]

and the command of the same form, without the dot:

  stb <probe> dec|oct|lin <points> <fstart> <fstop> [localgnd=<node>] [diag=1]

  <probe>     a voltage source (section 2) or <instance>:<terminal>
              (section 3)
  dec, oct    <points> per decade or per octave, as for .ac
  lin         <points> in all
  <fstart>, <fstop>   in Hz
  localgnd=   the reference node of the probe (section 7)
  diag=1      more vectors in the plot (section 6); diag=0, which is
              the same as no diag, is accepted

localgnd= and diag= follow the sweep, in either order; if one is given
twice, the last counts. From the probe on, a comma separates the words
as a blank does: .stb x1:inn,dec,20,1,100meg,diag=1
The probe is written without quotes.

  .stb vprb dec 50 100 1g
  .stb x1:inn dec 20 1 100meg diag=1
  stb xamp.m3:g oct 10 1k 1meg localgnd=vss

The points of the sweep are those that .ac makes for the same words. The
values are checked more strictly than .ac checks them: a number of
points below 1, a start frequency that is not positive (negative for
lin), a stop frequency below the start and a missing value are errors.
So is a sweep that cannot be stepped through: one whose step is too
small to change the frequency, or one too wide or with too many points
to count. One point (<fstop> equal to <fstart>) and a lin sweep from
0 Hz are allowed, but give no margins.

The variable stb_version is set to 1 in every ngspice that has the
analysis. A script can test for it with $?stb_version.

When the analysis runs:

  - The command runs it at once.
  - A .stb card is run by "run", and in batch mode it behaves as an .ac
    card does: "ngspice -b -r file.raw" runs it, and "ngspice -b" runs
    it if the deck has a .print or .plot card or a .control section
    that runs it.
  - "run" does the stb analyses after all other analyses of the deck.
    Several .stb cards are run one after the other, the last card of the
    deck first; this is the order ngspice has for several cards of any
    analysis.

AC values of sources are ignored, also that of the probe: for the loop
gain a voltage source is a short and a current source is open. DC values
count as always.

The operating point is found as for .ac. The options noopac and
keepopinfo apply; with keepopinfo a third plot, "STB Operating Point",
is made together with the other two when the sweep is done. If a source
depends on "hertz", the operating point is solved again at each
frequency. The analysis works with both matrix solvers (.option klu).


2  A voltage source as the probe
--------------------------------

Place a 0 V source in a wire through which all of the feedback passes,
and name it:

  vprb out fb dc 0
  .stb vprb dec 50 100 1g

A source inside a subcircuit instance is named by its path, x1.vprb, or
by the name it has in the circuit, v.x1.vprb. The source may carry a DC
value; it is then part of the circuit as it is for any other analysis.

The positive node of the source is the node x at which the current is
injected and the voltage is taken, the negative node is y. Which way
round the source is written does not change the loop gain. The first
line that a run prints names the two nodes (it is one line in the
output and is folded here):

  stb: probe vprb (voltage source vprb, x = out, y = fb), 351 points,
  100 Hz to 1e+09 Hz

If the positive node is ground, the source is taken the other way round
and the line says "source reversed". A probe with one node on ground
does not see the loop, since nothing can be injected into ground: place
the probe in a wire that is not on ground, or see section 7.

Only a voltage source can be the probe in this form. A resistor, a
subcircuit instance or a transistor as the probe is an error, and the
message names the form of section 3 that could be meant.


3  A terminal of an instance as the probe
-----------------------------------------

  .stb x1:inn dec 50 100 100meg

<instance>:<terminal> names one terminal of an element or one port of a
subcircuit instance. ngspice inserts the 0 V source itself: the terminal
is taken off its net and put on a new node x, and the source is placed
between x (its positive node) and the net (y). Everything else on the
net stays where it is. For a port of a subcircuit instance this opens
the connection of that one instance; other instances of the same
subcircuit are not touched.

The instance:

  r1, x1              an element or an instance at the top of the deck
  x1.r3, xamp.xm4.m1  an element inside an instance, by the path of the
                      instances
  r.x1.r3             the same by the name the element has in the circuit
  xamp.xbias          an instance inside an instance

The terminal:

  a number            the position on the instance line, counted from 1.
                      A number is always a position. If a port of the
                      subcircuit has that number as its name, the reader
                      says which was taken:
                        stb: probe x1:2: 2 is taken as a position, not
                        as the port named 2
  a port name         for a subcircuit instance, the name that the
                      .subckt line gives to the port
  a letter            for a transistor, in the order of the nodes on its
                      line:
                        M (MOS)              d g s b
                        Q (BJT)              c b e s
                        J, Z (JFET, MESFET)  d g s
                      A letter beyond the nodes the line has is refused.

Names are not case sensitive.

The source is inserted when the netlist is read, not when the analysis
runs. ngspice therefore has to find the probe in the deck. It collects
the probes

  - of the .stb cards at the top level of the deck, and
  - of the stb commands in the .control section of the deck in which
    the probe is written out, also inside if, while, repeat, dowhile
    and foreach.

and prints one line for each terminal:

  stb: probe x1:inn inserted as v.x1#stb2 (x = x1#stb2, y = mid)

"reset" inserts the sources again. A stb command for a terminal that
was not collected, because the probe comes from a variable or was typed
at the prompt and is nowhere in the deck, is an error that says so; add
a .stb card for that terminal to the deck. (A command finds the source
if the same terminal was collected under another spelling, x1:2 for
x1:inn.)

The names: the new node is the name that the instance has in the
circuit, "#stb" and the position of the terminal (x1#stb2,
r.x1.r3#stb1); the source is "v." and the name of the node. Spellings
of the same terminal share one source. If the deck has "#stb" in an
element line already, the mark is lengthened (#stb#) so that no name of
the deck is met.

What the insertion does to the circuit:

  - Each inserted source adds two vectors to the plots of every
    analysis: its current, v.x1#stb2#branch, and the voltage of the new
    node, x1#stb2. "save nointernals" leaves out the node.
  - The source has 0 V, so the circuit is the same electrically. The
    results of other analyses are equal to rounding; they are not
    identical bit for bit, since the matrix has one more node and one
    more branch.
  - An .ic for the net is repeated for the new node, so that a
    transient analysis with uic starts as before. With it the new node
    gets a capacitor of 0 F to ground, c.x1#stb2. It carries no current
    and no charge; it is there because with .option klu an .ic needs an
    element on its node, and a terminal such as a controlling input of
    an E or G element is none.
  - The name of the source has a "#" in it. Commands that take the "#"
    for something else do not find it: "show v.x1#stb2" answers "No
    matching instances or models". Vectors are not affected:
    "print i(v.x1#stb2)" works.

Probes that are refused. The deck still loads; the reader prints
"stb: probe ... not inserted:" with the reason, and the analysis with
that probe fails with the same reason:

  - an instance that does not exist, a position the instance does not
    have, an unknown port name or letter;
  - R, C and L with a value that is an expression, E and G with an
    expression, and POLY sources: ngspice replaces these by other
    elements when it reads the deck;
  - the third position of an F or H element (the controlling source is
    a name, not a node), a coupling K, a code model instance (A);
  - a port that is a global node, and a port that has the same name as
    an earlier port of the same .subckt line;
  - a port of an instance of a subcircuit that is defined inside
    another subcircuit; name a terminal of one of its elements;
  - a port that leads to a digital port of a code model.

In all these cases a voltage source placed by hand (section 2) does the
job.

Where the card is written:

  - A .stb card belongs at the top level of the deck. Inside a .subckt
    definition it is ignored: its probe is not collected and it is not
    run. The reader says so in one line for each such card.
  - A .stb card in a branch of .if that is not taken does not run, but
    the source for its terminal is inserted all the same.


4  The plots
------------

A run makes two plots:

  STB Analysis (stb1, stb2, ...)
      frequency   the scale
      loopgain    complex

  STB Margins (stbmargin1, stbmargin2, ...)
      phasemargin, phasemarginfreq, gainmargin, gainmarginfreq
      one real value each, in degrees, Hz, dB, Hz

The loop-gain plot is the current plot after the run. The name of the
margins plot is left in the variable stb_marginplot:

  stb vprb dec 50 100 1g
  print {$stb_marginplot}.phasemargin

Use the variable rather than a name made from the number of the
loop-gain plot: the two numbers are equal only as long as plots are
destroyed in pairs. A run that writes its results to a file ("run
file.raw", or -r in batch mode) makes no plots; the two plots are in the
file, and stb_marginplot is removed.

The sign of the loop gain. With T the return ratio of the loop,

  loopgain = -T

This is the convention of the Spectre circuit simulator. A loop with
negative feedback has T positive at low frequency, so loopgain starts
with a phase of 180 degrees, and its phase where its magnitude is 0 dB
is the phase margin:

  plot vdb(loopgain)
  set units = degrees
  plot vp(loopgain)

Take T = -loopgain where a formula or a tool expects the return ratio:

  let t = -loopgain

save and .save do not apply to the two plots: their vectors are results
of the analysis, not nodes of the circuit, and they are always written
in full.


5  The margins and the lines of a run
-------------------------------------

The margins are read off the points of the sweep. Between two points
the magnitude in dB and the phase in degrees are taken as straight lines
over the logarithm of the frequency, so the margins are as exact as the
sweep is dense: use 20 to 50 points per decade around the crossings.

  phase margin   180 degrees plus the phase of T at the first frequency
                 at which |T| falls through 0 dB
  gain margin    minus |T| in dB at the first frequency at which the
                 phase of T falls through -180 degrees

The phase of T is made continuous along the sweep first. A margin that
does not exist is NaN in the margins plot (the four vectors are always
there), and the run says why. The lines of a run, with the numbers of an
example:

  stb: Phase margin = 47.477 deg at frequency = 1.24835e+06 Hz
  stb: Gain margin = 20.8377 dB at frequency = 5.03569e+06 Hz

  stb: no phase margin: the loop gain is still +19.0077 dB at the last
  frequency 80.6349 Hz
      The sweep ends before |T| has fallen to 0 dB. The last frequency
      is the last point of the sweep, which is not always <fstop>.

  stb: no phase margin: the loop gain is below 0 dB at every frequency
  of the sweep (at most -6.02077 dB, at 1 Hz)

  stb: no gain margin: the loop-gain phase never falls to 0 deg (it is
  90.0091 deg at the last frequency 1e+06 Hz)
      The phase of loopgain at 0 degrees is the phase of T at -180.

  stb: no margins: positive feedback at the start frequency (loop-gain
  phase 359.64 deg at 1 Hz): the phase lag of the return ratio at the
  first point exceeds 90 degrees; a loop with an ideal integrator
  triggers this
      The real part of T is negative at the first point. The loop has
      positive feedback there, or the sweep starts too high, or T has a
      phase of -90 degrees from the start, as with an ideal integrator
      in the loop, and falls just beyond it. No margin is given.

  stb: the loop gain crosses 0 dB at 3 frequencies; the margins are at
  the first falling one

  stb: the loop gain rises through 0 dB at 1.59163 Hz, before the
  crossing used for the margins

  stb: the loop gain is zero at every frequency: is the probe in a
  feedback loop?
      |T| is below 1e-12 everywhere.

  stb: no margins: the loop gain is exactly zero at 100 Hz
      One point at which T is exactly zero is enough: no margin is
      given, also if the crossings lie below that frequency. A sweep
      that runs on beyond the crossings gets there when the loop gain
      is lost in the rounding of the solution. For a loop with many
      poles that can be little more than a decade above the 0 dB
      crossing. Stop the sweep soon after the crossings.

  stb: no margins: the sweep has fewer than two points
  stb: no margins: the sweep does not start above 0 Hz
  stb: warning: the loop gain is not finite at 1 Hz (31 point(s)); no
  margins

(Each of these is one line in the output; they are folded here.)

Errors. An analysis that cannot run prints one line that begins with
"Error: stb:" and names the probe (where one was given), and makes no
plot; the current plot and stb_marginplot stay as they were. A .stb card
that cannot be read does not stop the deck from loading. It is reported
when the analysis runs, and the other analyses of the deck run. As with
any analysis that fails, "run" then reports the run as aborted and does
not evaluate the .meas cards of the deck; the plots of the analyses
that ran are there, and the meas command can be used on them.

An interrupt (Ctrl-C) ends the analysis that is running. No plot is
made, and the analysis cannot be resumed. One interrupt ends one
analysis: if "run" has more .stb cards to do, it goes on with the next.
"resume" after the interrupt does not continue the analysis; it starts
the run again from the beginning.


6  diag=1
---------

With diag=1 the loop-gain plot holds six more vectors:

  loopgain_normal, loopgain_reverse
      The two parts of the loop gain, which sum to loopgain: the part of
      the loop that is controlled by the voltage on the x side of the
      probe, and the part that is controlled from the y side. A loop
      that carries signal one way only has one of them at zero. Writing
      the probe the other way round exchanges them.

  stb_i1, stb_v1
      the current in the probe and the voltage of node x for a unit
      voltage in series with the probe
  stb_i2, stb_v2
      the same for a unit current into node x

The loop gain is made of the four responses as

  K = 2 (stb_i1 stb_v2 - stb_v1 stb_i2) + stb_v1 + stb_i2
  loopgain = -K / (1 - K)

In a raw file the four responses are named as any current or voltage is:
i(stb_i1), v(stb_v1), i(stb_i2), v(stb_v2).


7  localgnd=<node>
------------------

Without it the injected current returns to ground and the voltage of
node x is taken against ground. That is right for a circuit that is
built on ground. For a loop that is built on a node of its own, name
that node:

  .stb vprb dec 20 1 1meg localgnd=ref

The current then returns to ref and the voltage of x is taken against
ref. If the positive node of the probe is ref, the source is taken the
other way round, as for ground; the node that is excited may then be
ground. localgnd=0 gives the same results as no localgnd (the first
line of the run then names "localgnd = 0"). A node that does not exist
is an error.

localgnd has been checked against closed forms only. It has not been
verified against another simulator.


8  .print, .plot, meas and .meas
--------------------------------

  .print stb vdb(loopgain) vp(loopgain)
  .plot stb vdb(loopgain)

"stb" on these cards selects the loop-gain plot, not the margins plot.

The meas command takes a loop-gain plot as it takes an ac plot. The
measurement is written with "ac":

  stb vprb dec 50 100 1g
  meas ac fu when vdb(loopgain)=0 fall=1
  meas ac lowdb find vdb(loopgain) at=100

The measurements phase_margin and gain_margin expect the return ratio,
not the loop gain. Give them T:

  let t = -loopgain
  meas ac pm phase_margin t
  meas ac gm gain_margin t

There is no ".meas stb" card. The .meas cards that a deck has for its
other analyses are evaluated by "run" as they are without a .stb card,
unless a .stb card fails (section 5).


9  What the analysis does not do
--------------------------------

  - A device as the probe (the loop gain "of" a transistor). Name one of
    its terminals.
  - Differential and common-mode loops.
  - Sweeps of anything but the frequency. Use a loop in a .control
    section, as examples/stb/stb-montecarlo.cir does.
  - Periodic operating points.

The margins have been checked against the closed forms of textbook
loops. They have not been compared with the results of another
simulator.
